        "root_concepts": "[social, studies]",
        "section": "philosophy-of-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Social Studies of Science: SSK, Laboratory Studies, Actor-Network Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "From the 1970s onward, a new approach to studying science emerged: instead of asking what rational standards ought to govern science (Popper, Lakatos), ask how science actually works as a social institution. The Strong Programme in sociology of scientific knowledge (SSK) and laboratory studies made the production of scientific facts itself the object of social analysis — not just institutional norms but the cognitive content of science."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md#003",
      "path": [
        "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:philosophy-of-science:08-social-studies, git-history:philosophy-of-science:08-social-studies]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy-of-science/08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md",
        "concepts": "[social, studies]",
        "current_path": "philosophy-of-science/08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy-of-science:social-studies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy-of-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[social, studies]",
        "section": "philosophy-of-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Social Studies of Science: SSK, Laboratory Studies, Actor-Network Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|               THE SOCIAL STUDIES TRADITION                       |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  MERTONIAN SOCIOLOGY (1940s–60s)                                |\n|  Studies NORMS of science: universalism, communism,             |\n|  disinterestedness, organized skepticism (CUDOS)                |\n|  NOT the content of knowledge itself                            |\n|              |                                                   |\n|              v (Kuhn's Structure opens the door: 1962)          |\n|  STRONG PROGRAMME (Edinburgh, 1970s)                            |\n|  Studies the CAUSES of scientific beliefs.                      |\n|  True and false beliefs explained by same sociological methods. |\n|  Symmetry principle: no privileged explanation for \"correct\"    |\n|  beliefs.                                                        |\n|              |                                                   |\n|              v (moves from institution to lab bench)            |\n|  LABORATORY STUDIES (1970s–80s)                                  |\n|  Ethnographic study of what scientists actually do              |\n|  Latour/Woolgar: facts constructed through inscription devices   |\n|              |                                                   |\n|              v (extends to technology, policy, artifacts)       |\n|  ACTOR-NETWORK THEORY (1980s–90s)                               |\n|  Latour/Callon: networks of human + non-human \"actants\"         |\n|  Science as practical achievement, not revealed truth           |\n|                                                                  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
