      },
      "text": "### The Symmetry Principle"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md#010",
      "path": [
        "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:philosophy-of-science:08-social-studies, git-history:philosophy-of-science:08-social-studies]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy-of-science/08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md",
        "concepts": "[social, studies]",
        "current_path": "philosophy-of-science/08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy-of-science:social-studies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy-of-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[social, studies]",
        "section": "philosophy-of-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Social Studies of Science: SSK, Laboratory Studies, Actor-Network Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The symmetry principle is SSK's most controversial claim and its most important methodological innovation. Before SSK, sociologists explained *false* beliefs sociologically (Newton believed in absolute space because of his religious views; scientists rejected continental drift because of professional conservatism) but explained *true* beliefs as simply tracking reality."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md#011",
      "path": [
        "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:philosophy-of-science:08-social-studies, git-history:philosophy-of-science:08-social-studies]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy-of-science/08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md",
        "concepts": "[social, studies]",
        "current_path": "philosophy-of-science/08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy-of-science:social-studies",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy-of-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[social, studies]",
        "section": "philosophy-of-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Social Studies of Science: SSK, Laboratory Studies, Actor-Network Theory",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Bloor says: that's asymmetric and unscientific. Both true and false beliefs have social causes. Explaining why physicists accepted quantum mechanics requires the same kind of sociological analysis as explaining why they initially rejected continental drift."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md#012",
      "path": [
        "08-SOCIAL-STUDIES.md"
      ],
